          <a href="#short-answer" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. The Short Answer</a>
          <a href="#signs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Signs You Need It</a>
          <a href="#soil-moisture" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Soil Moisture</a>
          <a href="#by-season" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Cool vs Warm Season</a>
          <a href="#how-often" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. How Often</a>
          <a href="#overseeding" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Aerate and Overseed</a>
          <a href="#the-day" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Getting the Day Right</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Aerate a cool-season lawn (bluegrass, tall fescue, ryegrass) in late summer to early fall, roughly mid-August to early October, with early spring as a distant second choice. Aerate a warm-season lawn (Bermuda, zoysia, St. Augustine) in late spring to early summer, after full green-up, when it is growing hard. In both cases do it when the soil is moist but not soggy, and only when the lawn actually shows signs of compaction; most lawns need it once a year at most, and healthy lawns on good soil can go two or three years between passes.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: Aerate at the Start of Peak Growth
          </h2>

          <p>
            Core aeration pulls thousands of finger-sized plugs of soil out of the lawn. Each hole lets air, water and fertilizer reach the root zone and gives roots room to expand into soil that was packed too tight. It is also a small wound. The grass around each hole has to grow into it, so the right time to aerate is when the grass is entering its strongest growth of the year and has several weeks of good conditions ahead.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Grass type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best window</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Second choice</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Avoid</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cool-season (bluegrass, tall fescue, ryegrass)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mid-August to early October</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Early spring, once growth starts</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Summer heat and drought, frozen or dormant turf</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Warm-season (Bermuda, zoysia, St. Augustine, centipede)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Late spring to early summer, after full green-up</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mid-summer with irrigation</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Spring green-up, fall approaching dormancy</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For cool-season grass, early fall wins on every count. Soil is still warm from summer, which drives root growth, while cooler air takes the stress off leaf tissue. Most weeds are winding down rather than germinating, so the open holes are less likely to fill with crabgrass. And it is the best window for overseeding, which is the single most useful thing to do right after aerating.
          </p>

          <p>
            For warm-season grass, late spring to early summer is the equivalent window. Bermuda and zoysia grow fastest when days are long and hot, so they close holes within a couple of weeks. Aerating them in early spring, before they are fully out of dormancy, or in fall as they slow down, leaves holes open for weeks and gives weeds an easy start. Our <a href="/best-lawn-aerator/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best lawn aerator picks</a> cover the tools that pull real cores in either season.
          </p>

          <p>
            One honest point up front: aeration is not a yearly ritual every lawn needs. It fixes compaction and helps manage thatch. If your soil is loose, water soaks in, and the grass is dense, you can skip it this year without losing anything. The next section explains how to tell.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="signs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Does Your Lawn Actually Need Aerating?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single best test costs nothing. Take a long screwdriver, about six to eight inches, and push it into the lawn the day after a good watering or rain. In healthy, uncompacted soil it slides in most of the way with moderate hand pressure. If it stops after an inch or two, or you have to lean your weight on it, the soil is compacted and the lawn will benefit from aeration. Test several spots, because compaction is usually patchy.
          </p>

          <p>
            The lawn itself also tells you. Water that puddles or runs off rather than soaking in, grass that thins along paths and play areas, and turf that browns quickly in dry spells despite regular watering are all classic signs. Heavy clay soil compacts easily and almost always benefits. So do lawns on new construction lots, where heavy equipment packed the subsoil and builders often spread only a thin layer of topsoil.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Thatch and layered sod</h3>

          <p>
            Cut a small wedge out of the lawn with a trowel and look at the profile. A spongy brown layer more than half an inch thick between the green blades and the soil is excess thatch, and core aeration helps break it down by mixing soil into it. If the layer is much thicker, our guide on <a href="/when-to-dethatch-lawn/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">when to dethatch your lawn</a> covers whether you need a dethatcher instead. Also look for a distinct line where sod was laid over different soil; that layer blocks water and roots, and aeration punches through it.
          </p>

          <p>
            What aeration will not fix is worth knowing too. It does not cure grass in deep shade, it does not correct a lawn mowed too short, and it does not replace fertilizer or water. If your screwdriver goes in easily and the lawn is still thin, look at sunlight, mowing height, soil fertility and grass variety first. Paying for aeration on loose, sandy soil is one of the more common ways homeowners waste money on lawn care.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="soil-moisture" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Wet Should the Soil Be When You Aerate?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Moisture decides whether an aerator pulls real cores or just bounces across the surface. In dry, hard soil, even a heavy core aerator only penetrates an inch or so and produces short, crumbly plugs. The goal is plugs two to three inches long, and that takes soil moist enough for the tines to sink in. The usual guidance is to water about an inch one or two days before aerating, or to wait a day after a soaking rain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Too wet is a problem too. Saturated soil smears rather than coring cleanly, plugs stick inside the tines, and a heavy machine can actually compact the surface further and leave ruts. If your footprints leave water in them or mud sticks to your shoes, wait a day or two. The ideal is the same feel you would want for digging a garden bed: moist and crumbly, not dusty and not sticky.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE TEST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">If a screwdriver goes in easily, the aerator will too</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Use the same screwdriver test the day before you aerate. If it slides in six inches with firm hand pressure, the soil is ready. If it stops short, water more. If water seeps around it when you pull it out, wait for the soil to drain.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Moisture is also why the seasonal windows work. Late summer and early fall often bring the first steady rains after summer dry spells, and late spring in the South brings enough rain and irrigation to keep soil workable. Trying to aerate in the middle of a summer drought means fighting baked soil, and the stressed grass will struggle to recover from the damage.
          </p>

          <p>
            After aerating, water again within a day or two, especially if you have overseeded or fertilized. The holes help that water soak in deeply, which is a large part of the point. Leave the cores where they fall; they dry and break down in one to two weeks, and running the mower over them once they are dry speeds that up.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="by-season" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Why Fall for Cool-Season and Late Spring for Warm-Season?
          </h2>

          <p>
            It is the same rule applied to two different grasses: aerate at the start of the grass's best growing period. Cool-season grasses grow best when air temperatures are roughly 60 to 75 degrees, which happens in spring and fall. Of the two, fall is better because the grass has a long stretch of favorable weather ahead, rather than the summer heat that follows spring.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spring aeration on cool-season lawns is not wrong, just riskier. Holes opened in March or April coincide with crabgrass germination, which starts once soil holds around 55 degrees, and the open soil gives those seeds an easy start. Spring aeration can also complicate a pre-emergent herbicide program. If you do aerate in spring, do it early, as soon as the grass is actively growing, and plan your crabgrass control around it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Warm-season grasses grow best at roughly 80 to 95 degrees, so their window runs from late spring, after the lawn has fully greened up and been mowed a couple of times, through early summer. Aerating then gives the grass the long, hot days it needs to spread back over the holes. Avoid late summer and fall, when warm-season grass is slowing toward dormancy, and avoid spring green-up, when it is still drawing on stored reserves.
          </p>

          <p>
            In the transition zone, where tall fescue and Bermuda or zoysia share space, aerate for the grass you are trying to favor. A fall aeration and overseeding pushes the fescue; a late-spring aeration helps the warm-season grass spread. Doing both every year mostly helps whichever grass is stronger in your yard, which is often not the one you planted.
          </p>

          <p>
            Regional timing shifts the calendar by a few weeks. In the far north, the cool-season window may close by mid-September, since you want about four weeks of growth before the first hard frost. In the transition zone it can run into October. For warm-season lawns along the Gulf Coast, May through July is typical, while a Bermuda lawn further north might wait until late May or June.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-often" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Often Should You Aerate?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Frequency depends on soil and use, not on a calendar. Heavy clay soils and lawns with heavy traffic, such as kids, dogs or a regular backyard soccer game, often benefit from aeration once a year. Average lawns on loam soils with moderate use usually do fine every two to three years. Sandy soils rarely compact and may never need routine aeration at all.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Situation</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical frequency</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Heavy clay, high traffic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Once a year</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">New construction lot, first few years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Once a year, sometimes twice</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Loam soil, moderate use</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Every 2-3 years</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sandy soil, light use</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Only when the screwdriver test fails</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Thatch building above half an inch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Once a year until under control</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Some lawn care programs on very compacted soils aerate twice a year, once in spring and once in fall for cool-season grass. That can make sense in the first few years on a badly compacted lot, but for most homeowners it is overkill. Re-run the screwdriver test each year before deciding. If it slides in easily, skip aeration and spend the money on seed or fertilizer instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use a core aerator, not a spike aerator, if you are aerating for compaction. Spike aerators and spiked shoes push holes into the soil without removing anything, which can compress the soil around each hole. Core aerators remove plugs and relieve compaction, which is why extension services consistently recommend them. Spikes have a place on loose soil before seeding, but they are not a fix for hard ground.
          </p>

          <p>
            One pass rarely covers enough area. A typical core aerator pulls holes a few inches apart, and the goal most turf specialists cite is roughly 20 to 40 holes per square foot. On a compacted lawn that usually means two passes in different directions. Two passes once a year beat a single pass twice a year.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="overseeding" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Should You Overseed Right After Aerating?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, if the lawn is at all thin, and particularly for cool-season grasses. Aeration followed by overseeding is one of the most effective lawn renovation combinations available to a homeowner. The holes give seed protected pockets with good soil contact and moisture, and new seedlings growing in them are less exposed than seed lying on a hard surface. Early fall is the ideal time for both.
          </p>

          <p>
            Order matters. Mow a little shorter than usual, aerate in two directions, then spread seed right away while the holes are open. Apply a starter fertilizer if your soil test supports it, and water lightly and often until the seed germinates. Our guide on <a href="/how-long-does-it-take-grass-seed-to-grow/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long grass seed takes to grow</a> covers watering schedules and germination times by species, and our <a href="/best-grass-seed/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best grass seed</a> guide covers which varieties suit sun, shade and traffic.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch your herbicides. Most crabgrass pre-emergents stop grass seed from germinating as well, typically for weeks to months depending on the product, so do not apply one before or right after overseeding. A few products are labeled for use at seeding, and the label is the final word. Broadleaf herbicides should also wait until the new grass has been mowed several times.
          </p>

          <p>
            Aeration also pairs well with fertilizer. Nutrients reach the root zone more directly through the holes, so early fall is an efficient time to aerate and feed in the same week. Our guide on <a href="/when-to-fertilize-lawn/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">when to fertilize your lawn</a> explains why that early-fall feeding is the most important of the year for cool-season grass. A <a href="/best-broadcast-spreader/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">broadcast spreader</a> handles both seed and fertilizer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Warm-season lawns are a different case. Bermuda and zoysia are usually spread by their own runners after aeration rather than overseeded, and many warm-season grasses are established from sod or plugs rather than seed. If you do seed Bermuda, wait until soil is consistently above 65 degrees in late spring.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="the-day" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Do You Get the Most Out of Aeration Day?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Preparation makes most of the difference. Mark sprinkler heads, shallow irrigation lines, buried dog fence wire and cable runs before you start; a core aerator's tines go two to three inches deep and can snap a sprinkler head in a second. Mow a day or two before aerating. Water an inch one or two days ahead so the soil is moist but not soggy, as described above.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rental core aerators are heavy and powerful, often well over 150 pounds, and they pull hard. Two people are usually needed to load one into a truck, and turning one on a slope takes some strength. For small lawns, a manual core aerator works but is slow, tiring work on clay. For mid-sized lawns, tow-behind core aerators for a riding mower are a reasonable middle ground if you aerate yearly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Run the machine in straight passes, like mowing, then make a second set of passes at an angle to the first. Lift the tines when turning to avoid tearing turf. Check plugs as you go: if they are under an inch long, the soil is too dry or the machine is not heavy enough. Leave the cores on the lawn to break down. They return soil and microbes to the surface and help decompose thatch.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hiring a lawn service is worth considering if you do not own a truck to move a rental machine or your lawn has slopes. Ask whether they use core aerators, how many passes they make, and whether they mark irrigation heads. A single quick pass with widely spaced holes is the most common shortcut. Whatever route you choose, get the timing and moisture right first; those two factors matter more than the machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Aerating bone-dry soil</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tines barely penetrate hard, dry ground, so you get short, crumbly plugs and little benefit. Water about an inch one or two days before aerating, or wait for a soaking rain, and confirm with the screwdriver test. Plugs two to three inches long tell you the soil was ready for the machine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a spike aerator on compacted clay</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Spikes push soil aside rather than removing it, which can compress the soil around each hole and do little to relieve compaction. For hard clay or high-traffic lawns, use a core aerator that pulls plugs. Save spike tools for loosening the surface of already loose soil before seeding.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Aerating warm-season grass in fall</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bermuda, zoysia and St. Augustine slow down as fall arrives, so holes stay open for weeks and weeds move in. Aerate warm-season lawns in late spring to early summer, after full green-up, when the grass is growing hard enough to close the holes within a couple of weeks.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting down pre-emergent before overseeding</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most crabgrass pre-emergents stop grass seed from sprouting along with the weeds. If you aerate and overseed, skip the pre-emergent or use a product specifically labeled as safe at seeding. Otherwise you have paid for seed and aeration that will never fill in the lawn.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting to mark sprinkler heads</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Core aerator tines go two to three inches deep and will snap sprinkler heads and shallow irrigation lines. Flag every head, valve box, buried pet fence and shallow cable before starting. A few minutes with marking flags saves a repair bill and a broken zone in the middle of fall.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I remove the plugs after aerating?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Leave the cores on the lawn. They dry out and break down within one to two weeks, returning soil and microbes to the surface where they help decompose thatch. Raking them up wastes time and removes that benefit. If they bother you, run the mower over them once they are dry, or drag a mat across the lawn to break them up.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you aerate a lawn too much?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, though it is uncommon. Aerating several times a year on loose or sandy soil damages roots and opens the lawn to weeds without relieving any compaction. Use the screwdriver test to decide each year. Once or twice a year is the realistic ceiling for compacted clay, and many lawns only need it every two or three years.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I mow right after aerating?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can mow a few days after aerating, once the cores have dried. Mowing too soon on wet plugs smears soil across the lawn and onto the mower deck. If you overseeded, wait until the new grass reaches mowing height, usually three to four inches, before mowing again, and use a sharp blade so seedlings are cut rather than pulled.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it better to aerate before or after rain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A day after a soaking rain is ideal, because the soil is moist enough for tines to penetrate but has drained enough not to smear. Aerating during or just after a downpour risks muddy, smeared holes and ruts. If no rain is coming, water about an inch one or two days before aerating to get the same effect.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does aerating help with thatch?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, moderately. Core aeration mixes soil from the plugs into the thatch layer, which brings in the microbes that break thatch down. Yearly aeration can keep thatch in check on lawns where it is building slowly. If thatch is already well over half an inch, a dethatcher or power rake removes it faster than aeration alone.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I fertilize after aerating?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Fertilizing right after aerating is efficient because nutrients reach the root zone through the holes. For cool-season lawns, aerating in early fall lines up with the most important feeding of the year. If you are also overseeding, use a starter fertilizer where your soil test and local rules allow, then return to the normal fall schedule.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I aerate in summer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For cool-season lawns, avoid it; summer heat and drought stress the grass, and holes dry out the root zone. For warm-season lawns, early summer is part of the ideal window, and mid-summer is acceptable with good irrigation. If you aerate any lawn in summer, make sure you can keep it watered afterward so the grass recovers quickly.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

