        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The best time to aerate is when your grass is entering its strongest growth: late summer to early fall for cool-season lawns, late spring to early summer for warm-season lawns. Water a day or two ahead so the soil is moist, use a core aerator, make two passes, and leave the plugs where they fall.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Most importantly, aerate because the lawn needs it, not because the calendar says so. If the screwdriver slides in easily and water soaks in, skip it this year. If it does not, aerate in the right window, overseed thin areas while the holes are open, and feed the lawn on schedule. That combination fixes more tired lawns than any product on the shelf.
          </p>
        </div>

